        "worked_solution": "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} & =4{v}_{1}+5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} \\\\  & =4({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =4(0)+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=T(5{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}].$",
